    "result_text": "All tests pass. The change is complete and the suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `customerCode` assignment (`<LAST3>-<NNNN>`) for owner creation:\n\n1. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a persisted `customerCode` field (`@Column(name = \"customer_code\")`) with getter/setter.\n\n2. **Schema** (`db/hsqldb/schema.sql` used by tests, plus `db/h2/schema.sql` for dev) \u2014 added a `customer_code VARCHAR(20)` column to the `owners` table so it round-trips through the DB and is readable via `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `customerCode` property to the `Owner` response schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct maps it automatically by name from the entity.\n\n4. **`AssignCustomerCode.java`** (new OfficeFloor function) \u2014 computes `LAST3` (upper-cased first three letters of `lastName`, safely handling short names) and `NNNN` as a zero-padded 4-digit sequence equal to `ownerRepository.findAll().size() + 1`, then mutates the built `Owner` in place.\n\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted the `assignCustomerCode` step between `build` and `save`, under the same `transaction` governance.\n\nBecause the count is read within the same request transaction (and the test's outer `@Transactional` keeps prior creates visible), the global sequence increments by exactly one per created owner, satisfying `coreFormatAndGlobalSequence`.",
